  <section class="book" aria-labelledby="book-title">
    <img src="/assets/maps/korti-to-khartum/cover_front.webp" width="901" height="1351" alt="Cover of From Korti to Khartum by Sir Charles W. Wilson, AETERNUS Green Series" loading="lazy">
    <div class="blurb">
      <h2 id="book-title">The Book</h2>
      <p class="caps">Sudan, 1885. Khartoum is besieged. Time is running out.</p>
      <p>Across the Bayuda Desert, the British Desert Column drives towards the Nile in a desperate attempt to reach General Gordon. Among its officers is Sir Charles William Wilson—Royal Engineer, intelligence chief and, after the mortal wounding of Sir Herbert Stewart, commander in the field. He fights through Abu Klea and Abu Kru, reaches the Nile, and boards Gordon’s armed steamers for the final dash to Khartoum.</p>
      <p>He arrives on 28 January 1885.</p>
      <p>Two days too late.</p>
      <blockquote>“The record of one of the most remarkable enterprises in military history.”<cite>— The Times</cite></blockquote>
      <p>Sir Charles W. Wilson, <em>From Korti to Khartum</em>. The AETERNUS edition of the journal first published in 1885, with a portrait of the author, a new preface and afterword, and all the plans and sketch maps printed in the text of the original. Paperback, hardcover and e-book — coming soon.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="note" aria-labelledby="about-map">
    <h2 id="about-map">About the Map</h2>
    <p>The map is newly drawn. Route, camps, wells and dates follow Wilson’s journal and the sketch map published with it in 1885; they are set on a modern survey of the ground, so the positions in the desert are good to within a few miles. Place names keep the spellings of Wilson’s day.</p>
    <p>The Nile is drawn in its present course. The Merowe reservoir, flooded in 2009, is left out, and the Fourth Cataract is shown as the open river the soldiers knew.</p>
    <p>Relief: SRTM (NASA) via AWS Terrain Tiles. River: © <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright" rel="noopener">OpenStreetMap contributors</a>, ODbL.</p>
  </section>

